{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MFBhGxo6rcuAW2c9jLRJ1d","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the nsight kernel summary. Let me analyze the top kernels:\n\n1. **apc_apply_bus_kernel**: 20.2% of GPU time, 611ms, 583 instances. This is GKR input evaluation kernel.\n2. **evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<true>**: 13.6% of GPU time, 412ms, 486 instances. Also GKR input evaluation.\n3. **zerocheck_ntt_evaluate_constraints_coset_parallel_kernel<true,false>**: 7.9%, 238ms, 539 instances. Round 0 constraints.\n\n\n4. **poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel**: 7.6%, 231ms, 25 instances. This handles the Merkle tree hashing during trace commit.\n5. **logup_r0_ntt_eval_interactions_coset_parallel_kernel<true,false>**: 7.1%, 214ms, 396 instances. Round 0 interactions evaluation.\n6. **calculate_zero_hash**: 3.6%, 109ms, 584 instances. Also part of the Merkle tree construction in trace commit.\n\nNow let me check the NVTX range data to understand the overall execution flow.\n\nLooking at the big picture across both runs, the STARK phase excluding trace commit takes about 1720ms total. The constraints dominate at 1240ms, with LogUp GKR consuming the lion's share at 755ms. The openings and trace commit phases each contribute around 250ms and 227ms respectively. On the GPU side, the kernel profiling shows apc_apply_bus_kernel and evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel are the heaviest hitters for GKR input processing, while zerocheck_ntt_evaluate_constraints_coset_parallel handles the Round 0 work.\n\nLooking deeper into the kernel breakdown, I see poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes and logup_r0_ntt_eval_interactions_coset_parallel are significant contributors to the trace commit and Round 0 phases. The GKR input evaluation kernels alone account for about 1082ms of raw GPU time, but with 8-thread multi-streaming we're achieving roughly 1.43x parallelism, bringing the wall clock time down to 755ms—so the multi-streaming strategy is actually delivering meaningful speedup. For GKR at 755ms wall clock with 1082ms of GPU kernel time, we're only hitting 1.43x parallelism—well below what 8 threads should provide, which aligns with the memory manager contention issues. The biggest opportunity is GKR itself: if we could reach 4x parallelism, we'd cut it down to ~271ms and save ~484ms total. Round 0 is already doing better at 1.62x parallelism, so the gains there would be more modest.\n\nThe real bottleneck appears to be the MemoryManager mutex preventing GKR input eval from scaling—multiple reports point to this as the limiting factor, and fixing per-thread buffer pools could unlock the parallelism we're leaving on the table.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4176,"cache_read_input_tokens":61988,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4176},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"39e8685b-d752-4f1d-86a0-2956436bbd44","uuid":"961a41a1-6c60-41d8-a0ef-c3c2630c0509"}
