{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01T2FRaKuHbXH8iSaDfDUjEz","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have all the information I need to provide a comprehensive report. Let me create the final summary:\n\n## GKR Input Evaluation Code Path & GPU Memory Allocation Analysis\n\nI've thoroughly analyzed the multi-threaded GKR input evaluation code and the underlying MemoryManager. Here's the complete picture:\n\n### 1. **High-Level Control Flow: `log_gkr_input_evals`**\n\n**File**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs` (lines 215-297)\n\nThe function implements a two-phase parallel evaluation pattern:\n\n- **Phase 1 (lines 232-252)**: Build work items from `trace_interactions` and sort by descending height for load balancing\n- **Barrier (line 255)**: `current_stream_sync()` ensures all prior GPU work is visible to worker threads\n- **Phase 2 (lines 257-292)**: Process AIRs in parallel:\n  - Single thread if < 100 work items (lines 264-269)\n  - Multi-threaded otherwise using `std::thread::scope` with `NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS` threads (default 8, line 30)\n  - Each worker processes a chunk of AIRs sequentially within its thread\n  - Worker thread syncs before returning (line 282)\n\n### 2. **Per-AIR GPU Memory Allocations: `process_gkr_input_air`**\n\n**File**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs` (lines 105-210)\n\nEach AIR processed calls `process_gkr_input_air`, which allocates the following GPU buffers:\n\n| Allocation | File:Line | Type | Triggering Condition | Buffer Capacity |\n|-----------|-----------|------|----------------------|-----------------|\n| `null_preprocessed` | gkr_input.rs:110 | `DeviceBuffer::<F>::new()` | Always (may unused) | 0 (null) |\n| `d_public_values` | gkr_input.rs:135 | `to_device()` → `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity()` | If `air_ctx.public_values` not empty | `public_values.len()` |\n| `d_partition_ptrs` resize | gkr_input.rs:145 | `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(partition_ptrs.len())` | If existing buffer too small | `num_partitions` (u64 pointers) |\n| `intermediates` | gkr_input.rs:153 | `DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity()` | Always allocated | `(TASK_SIZE × buffer_size)` if `buffer_size > 10`; else `1` |\n| `tmp` resize | gkr_input.rs:167 | `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(required)` | If `height != lifted_height` AND existing buffer too small | `height × num_interactions` |\n\nAll allocations eventually call `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity()` which invokes `d_malloc()` → `MEMORY_MANAGER.lock().manager.d_malloc()`.\n\n### 3. **The MemoryManager: Mutex & Allocation Strategy**\n\n**File**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/mod.rs`\n\n#### Global Singleton:\n```rust\nstatic MEMORY_MANAGER: OnceLock<Mutex<MemoryManager>> = OnceLock::new();  // line 30\n```\n\n#### Public Allocation Interface:\n```rust\npub fn d_malloc(size: usize) -> Result<*mut c_void, MemoryError> {\n    let manager = MEMORY_MANAGER.get().unwrap();\n    let mut manager = manager.lock().map_err(|_| MemoryError::LockError)?;  // line 143\n    manager.d_malloc(size)\n}\n```\n\n**Each allocation in the per-AIR loop acquires the mutex lock, holds it during the actual d_malloc call, then releases it.**\n\n#### Allocation Strategy (lines 71-99):\n1. **For small allocations** (< pool page_size): Direct `cudaMallocAsync()` on the per-thread stream\n2. **For large allocations** (>= page_size): Virtual memory pool allocation via `pool.malloc_internal()`\n\nThe MemoryManager internally maintains:\n- `allocated_ptrs`: HashMap tracking small allocations (cudaMallocAsync)\n- `pool`: VirtualMemoryPool with free regions, defragmentation, and zombie tracking\n- `current_size` / `max_used_size`: Memory usage tracking\n\n### 4. **Virtual Memory Pool: The Deferred Free Pattern**\n\n**File**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/vm_pool.rs`\n\nThe VirtualMemoryPool uses sophisticated defragmentation:\n\n- **Free regions** tracked in BTreeMap with per-stream events (line 119)\n- **Zombie regions** for deferred unmapping (line 128) — old VA regions kept mapped until GPU work completes\n- **Best-fit allocation** preferring current stream, then completed events (lines 321-350)\n- **Defragmentation** (lines 510-675): Reuses holes, coalesces adjacent regions, remaps fragmented pages to contiguous VA space using double-mapping\n\n### 5. **Multi-Threading & Mutex Serialization Points**\n\nThe multi-threaded GKR input evaluation has these serialization points:\n\n1. **Before worker threads start (line 255)**: `current_stream_sync()` blocks main thread until default stream drains\n   \n2. **During per-AIR allocations**: Each call to `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity()` → `d_malloc()` acquires `Mutex<MemoryManager>` (lines 141-144):\n   - Lock held during: memory pool search, defragmentation decisions, virtual memory operations\n   - Lock held briefly across `cudaMallocAsync()` call on per-thread stream (line 77)\n   - **Per-thread streams** (`cudaStreamPerThread`) are CUDA's built-in per-thread mechanism, NOT manual streams\n\n3. **Within each worker thread (line 282)**: Worker thread calls `current_stream_sync()` before returning — syncs its per-thread stream\n\n4. **In MemoryManager deallocation (lines 150-154)**: DeviceBuffer Drop holds mutex during `d_free()`\n\n### 6. **Key Allocation Points with Line Numbers**\n\n**In `log_gkr_input_evals`:**\n- Line 228: `leaves = DeviceBuffer::<Frac<EF>>::with_capacity(total_leaves)` — main output buffer (shared across all workers, filled in-place)\n- Line 265 (single-threaded): `d_partition_ptrs = DeviceBuffer::<u64>::new()`\n- Line 266 (single-threaded): `tmp = DeviceBuffer::<Frac<EF>>::new()`\n- Line 277-278 (multi-threaded, spawned closure): Per-worker `d_partition_ptrs` and `tmp` created fresh in each OS thread\n\n**In `process_gkr_input_air` (called per AIR):**\n- Line 110: `null_preprocessed = DeviceBuffer::<F>::new()` (empty, no allocation)\n- Line 135: `d_public_values = air_ctx.public_values.to_device()` (allocates if public values exist)\n- Line 145: `*d_partition_ptrs = DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(partition_ptrs.len())` (resize if needed)\n- Line 153 or 155: `intermediates = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(...)` (always)\n- Line 167: `*tmp = DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(required)` (resize if height scaling needed)\n\n### 7. **Mutex Contention Pattern**\n\n**Critical insight**: The Mutex serializes **only the allocation/deallocation decisions and metadata updates**, not GPU execution:\n\n- All actual kernel launches occur **after** the lock is released\n- `cudaMallocAsync()` for small allocs doesn't block; it queues on per-thread stream (line 77)\n- Virtual pool operations under lock are fast (metadata updates only; no GPU sync within lock except defrag decisions)\n- Defragmentation can trigger `default_stream_wait()` (line 644 in vm_pool.rs) but only when needed and while lock is held\n\n**Multi-threaded workers don't contend heavily because**:\n1. Each worker has its own per-thread stream (CUDA feature)\n2. Each worker allocates independently within its AIR loop\n3. Mutex is held briefly per allocation (~microseconds)\n4. Most per-AIR allocations are **reused** buffers (`d_partition_ptrs`, `tmp`) rather than new allocations\n\nThis design achieves parallelism while maintaining thread-safe memory tracking."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"39e8685b-d752-4f1d-86a0-2956436bbd44","uuid":"d7e9ece5-7eef-4308-b0d5-bc377e19c61d","timestamp":"2026-04-13T03:38:25.845Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I need to understand the GKR input evaluation code path in this CUDA prover, focusing on:\n\n1. The `log_gkr_input_evals` function and how it processes AIRs (multi-stream pattern with per-thread CUDA streams)\n2. The `process_gkr_input_air` function — what GPU buffers it allocates per AIR\n3. How the MemoryManager works —