{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NKx1dwnpUnVhMaSeMe3kPv","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Excellent! Now I have all the information I need. Let me create a comprehensive deep-dive document:\n\n## GPU MemoryManager (VPMM) Deep-Dive Analysis\n\n### 1. MemoryManager Struct Definition & Architecture\n\n**Location:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/mod.rs` (lines 30-133)\n\n**Global Singleton:**\n```\nstatic MEMORY_MANAGER: OnceLock<Mutex<MemoryManager>> = OnceLock::new();\n```\n\n**Initialization:** Via `#[ctor::ctor]` function at program startup (line 40)\n\n**MemoryManager Structure:**\n```rust\npub struct MemoryManager {\n    pool: VirtualMemoryPool,              // The VPMM pool itself\n    allocated_ptrs: HashMap<NonNull<c_void>, usize>,  // Tracks small allocations\n    current_size: usize,                  // Current GPU memory used\n    max_used_size: usize,                 // Peak usage for metrics\n}\n```\n\n**Mutex Protection:**\n- Every allocation/deallocation goes through `Mutex::lock()` (lines 142-154)\n- Lock is acquired at the entry points `d_malloc()` and `d_free()`, held through the entire operation\n- Returns `MemoryError::LockError` if lock fails\n\n### 2. Allocation & Deallocation Flow\n\n**d_malloc() Flow (lines 71-99):**\n1. Check if size < `pool.page_size` (default 2MB)\n   - **Small allocations:** Use `cudaMallocAsync` (GPU stream-based async pool)\n   - Tracked in `allocated_ptrs` HashMap\n   - **Large allocations:** Round up to page boundary, use VPMM pool\n2. For VPMM allocations:\n   - Round size up to next multiple of `page_size`\n   - Call `pool.malloc_internal(rounded_size, stream_id)`\n3. Track in `current_size` and update `max_used_size`\n\n**d_free() Flow (lines 104-120):**\n1. Look up pointer in `allocated_ptrs` HashMap\n   - If found: use `cudaFreeAsync` (for small allocations)\n   - If not found: delegate to `pool.free_internal()` (for VPMM allocations)\n2. Decrement `current_size`\n\n**DeviceBuffer Integration (d_buffer.rs):**\n- `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity()` calls `d_malloc()` (line 68)\n- Drop impl calls `d_free()` (line 178)\n- **All device buffers always go through MemoryManager** — there's no bypass\n\n### 3. CUDA-Level Memory Management\n\n**Two-tier system:**\n\n**Tier 1: Small allocations (<2MB) — cudaMallocAsync**\n```c\nextern \"C\" {\n    fn cudaMallocAsync(dev_ptr: *mut *mut c_void, size: usize, stream: cudaStream_t) -> i32;\n    fn cudaFreeAsync(dev_ptr: *mut c_void, stream: cudaStream_t) -> i32;\n}\n```\n- Async pool managed by CUDA runtime\n- Per-stream operation (uses `cudaStreamPerThread`)\n- No virtual memory overhead\n\n**Tier 2: Large allocations (>=2MB) — VPMM (Virtual Pool Memory Manager)**\n- Uses CUDA virtual memory APIs (cuda.h, not cuda_runtime_api.h)\n- Implemented in `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-common/cuda/src/vpmm_shim.cu`\n\n**VPMM C++ Wrapper Functions:**\n- `_vpmm_check_support()` → `cuDeviceGetAttribute(CU_DEVICE_ATTRIBUTE_VIRTUAL_MEMORY_MANAGEMENT_SUPPORTED)`\n- `_vpmm_reserve()` → `cuMemAddressReserve()` — Reserve virtual address space (no physical memory yet)\n- `_vpmm_create_physical()` → `cuMemCreate()` — Allocate physical memory\n- `_vpmm_map()` → `cuMemMap()` — Map physical memory to virtual address\n- `_vpmm_unmap()` → `cuMemUnmap()` — Unmap (async, for double-mapping during defrag)\n- `_vpmm_set_access()` → `cuMemSetAccess()` — Set read/write permissions\n- `_vpmm_release()` → `cuMemRelease()` — Release physical memory handle\n- `_vpmm_release_va()` → `cuMemAddressFree()` — Release virtual address reservation\n\n**Key Architectural Points:**\n- VPMM supports **virtual-to-physical remapping** without deallocating\n- This enables **double-mapping during defragmentation** (old VA + new VA point to same physical pages temporarily)\n- Unmapped pages tracked as \"zombie regions\" with completion events for async cleanup\n\n### 4. VirtualMemoryPool: The Core VPMM Implementation\n\n**Location:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/vm_pool.rs`\n\n**Pool Metadata (lines 112-141):**\n```rust\npub struct VirtualMemoryPool {\n    roots: Vec<CUdeviceptr>,                              // VA roots (can grow)\n    active_pages: HashMap<CUdeviceptr, CUmemGenericAllocationHandle>,  // Currently mapped\n    free_regions: BTreeMap<CUdeviceptr, FreeRegionMeta>,  // Available for reuse\n    malloc_regions: HashMap<CUdeviceptr, usize>,          // Current allocations\n    unmapped_regions: BTreeMap<CUdeviceptr, usize>,       // \"Holes\" from remapping\n    zombie_regions: Vec<ZombieRegion>,                    // Old mappings awaiting event completion\n    free_num: usize,                                       // Counter for free region IDs\n    page_size: usize,                                      // Granularity (2MB default)\n    va_size: usize,                                        // Per-chunk VA reservation (8TB default)\n    device_id: i32,\n}\n```\n\n**Configuration (lines 36-87):**\n- Environment variables: `VPMM_PAGE_SIZE`, `VPMM_VA_SIZE`, `VPMM_PAGES`\n- Default: 8TB VA space, device's minimum granularity (typically 2MB)\n- Optional pre-allocation of pages at startup\n\n**Free Region Metadata:**\n```rust\nstruct FreeRegionMeta {\n    size: usize,\n    event: Arc<CudaEvent>,       // Records when freed\n    stream_id: CudaStreamId,       // Which stream freed it\n    id: usize,                     // Sequential ID for oldest-first ordering\n}\n```\n\n### 5. Pool Allocation Strategy\n\n**malloc_internal() - Three Phases (lines 243-292):**\n\n**Phase 0: Cleanup**\n- Call `cleanup_zombie_regions()` to finalize old unmapped VA\n- Checks if zombie events have completed, then calls `vpmm_unmap()`\n\n**Phase 1: Zero-cost fit**\n- Call `find_best_fit()` to locate free region without defragmentation\n- Prefers **same-stream** regions (no sync required)\n- Falls back to **completed regions from other streams** (no sync overhead)\n- Returns immediately if found\n\n**Phase 2: Defragmentation**\n- Call `defragment_or_create_new_pages()` if no fit found\n- May remap existing free regions to consolidate space\n- May allocate new physical pages if needed\n- Double-maps during remapping to avoid GPU work interruption\n\n**find_best_fit() Strategy (lines 321-351):**\n1. Filter regions >= requested size\n2. Priority 1: Current stream's smallest fit (no waiting)\n3. Priority 2: Completed regions from other streams (no waiting)\n4. Update region's stream_id if reused from another stream\n\n### 6. Defragmentation & Remapping\n\n**defragment_or_create_new_pages() (lines 510-675):**\n1. Find unmapped VA hole to hold coalesced pages\n2. Allocate new physical pages if free regions insufficient\n3. Select regions to remap (oldest first, avoiding current stream if possible)\n4. For cross-stream regions: call `default_stream_wait(&region.event)` to ensure completion\n5. Double-map selected regions to new VA via `remap_regions()`\n6. Old VA added to `zombie_regions` with event, awaiting async cleanup\n\n**remap_regions() (lines 687-749):**\n- Maps physical pages to new VA (old VA still mapped)\n- Calls `vpmm_set_access()` on new VA\n- Old VA becomes zombie, queued for later async unmap\n\n**Coalescing on Free (lines 382-420):**\n- When `free_internal()` marks region free, immediately attempts to merge with adjacent regions\n- Only merges regions on the **same stream** (important: prevents blocking other streams)\n- Records new event on the stream for async tracking\n\n### 7. Global State & Singletons\n\n**Global State (mod.rs):**\n```\nOnceLock<Mutex<MemoryManager>> MEMORY_MANAGER\n└── Mutex<MemoryManager>\n    ├── VirtualMemoryPool (no internal lock)\n    └── HashMap<NonNull<c_void>, usize>  // Small alloc tracking\n```\n\n**Thread-local State (stream.rs, line 111-113):**\n```\nthread_local! {\n    static CUDA_THREAD_CLEANUP: CudaThreadCleanup = ...\n}\n```\n- Marks if thread touched CUDA\n- Synchronizes default stream on thread exit\n\n**Per-Stream State:**\n- `CudaStreamId` extracted via `cudaStreamGetId(cudaStreamPerThread, &id)`\n- Used to partition free regions by stream for lock-free reuse\n\n### 8. Lock Contention Analysis\n\n**CRITICAL BOTTLENECK: The MemoryManager Mutex**\n\nEvery allocation/deallocation **must acquire the global Mutex** (lines 142-154):\n```rust\npub fn d_malloc(size: usize) -> Result<*mut c_void, MemoryError> {\n    let manager = MEMORY_MANAGER.get().unwrap();\n    let mut manager = manager.lock().map_err(|_| MemoryError::LockError)?;\n    manager.d_malloc(size)  // Lock held for entire operation\n}\n```\n\n**Lock Hold Time Cost:**\n- Small allocations: `cudaMallocAsync` call → typically ~microseconds\n- Large allocations: May include:\n  - BTreeMap lookups (O(log n) for free regions)\n  - Defragmentation (remap pages, record events) → ~milliseconds\n  - VA re-reservation if needed → potentially expensive\n\n**Contention Scenarios:**\n- Multiple threads allocating simultaneously → all queue on Mutex\n- One slow defragmentation holds lock, blocking all other threads\n- defragmentation triggers `default_stream_wait()` which synchronizes GPU → lock held during GPU stall\n\n**Per-Thread Buffer Reuse Opportunity:**\n- `find_best_fit()` prefers same-stream regions (line 334-340)\n- Same stream can reuse freed regions **without synchronization**\n- But still requires Mutex lock just to query the pool\n- **Cannot bypass lock** because `allocated_ptrs` HashMap is protected\n\n### 9. Stream-Based Optimization\n\n**Stream Partitioning (design, not lock-free):**\n- Free regions tagged with `stream_id` at free time\n- Reuse preference: same stream → other completed streams\n- Avoids cross-stream synchronization when possible\n- But all access still serialized by Mutex\n\n**Example Fast Path (same-stream reuse):**\n1. Thread A frees on Stream X → region marked for Stream X\n2. Thread A allocates on Stream X → immediately finds region (no wait/sync)\n3. **But:** Both operations still contend for Mutex lock\n\n### 10. Error Handling\n\n**MemoryError Variants:**\n- `LockError` — Mutex poisoned (lines 143, 152)\n- `OutOfMemory` — Not enough free pages even after defrag (line 288)\n- `InvalidPointer` — Pointer not in allocation table (line 368)\n- `ReserveFailed` — VA reservation failed (lines 445-448)\n- Plus variants from CudaError\n\n**Fallback Behavior:**\n- Small OOM retries don't cascade; caller must handle\n- VPMM OOM can happen mid-defragmentation\n\n### 11. Memory Tracking & Metrics\n\n**MemTracker Utility (lines 156-238):**\n```rust\npub struct MemTracker {\n    current: usize,  // Snapshot at creation\n    label: &'static str,\n}\n```\n- Records GPU memory usage at scope entry\n- `emit_metrics()` publishes to metrics subsystem:\n  - `gpu_mem.current_bytes` — Current usage\n  - `gpu_mem.local_peak_bytes` — Peak since last reset\n  - `gpu_mem.reserved_bytes` — Total in pool\n- Non-blocking (gracefully handles lock failures)\n\n### 12. Configuration\n\n**Environment Variables:**\n```\nVPMM_PAGE_SIZE      # Bytes, must be multiple of device granularity (default: device min)\nVPMM_VA_SIZE        # Bytes, default 8TB\nVPMM_PAGES          # Number of pages to pre-allocate (default: 0)\n```\n\n**Benefits of pre-allocation:**\n- Avoids initial `cuMemCreate` cost on first large allocation\n- Reduces peak lock contention at startup\n\n### 13. Key Files & Line References\n\n| File | Purpose | Key Functions |\n|------|---------|---|\n| `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/mod.rs` | Manager singleton, public API | `d_malloc`, `d_free`, `MemoryManager::new`, `MemTracker` |\n| `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/vm_pool.rs` | Pool logic, defrag, coalescing | `malloc_internal`, `free_internal`, `defragment_or_create_new_pages`, `remap_regions` |\n| `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/cuda.rs` | VPMM C wrappers | `vpmm_reserve`, `vpmm_create_physical`, `vpmm_map`, `vpmm_unmap` |\n| `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-common/cuda/src/vpmm_shim.cu` | CUDA C++ impl | `_vpmm_*` functions calling CUDA driver APIs |\n| `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-common/src/d_buffer.rs` | Typed device buffer wrapper | `with_capacity`, `Drop` → calls `d_malloc`/`d_free` |\n| `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-common/src/stream.rs` | Stream & event management | `cudaStreamPerThread`, `current_stream_id`, `CudaEvent` |\n\n### 14. Summary: Lock Cost & Optimization Potential\n\n**Cost of Taking MemoryManager Lock:**\n- **Minimum:** ~10-100 ns (uncontended lock acquisition)\n- **Small alloc fast path:** ~1-10 μs (Mutex + HashMap lookup + cudaMallocAsync)\n- **Large alloc no-defrag path:** ~10-100 μs (Mutex + BTreeMap lookup + VPMM page check)\n- **With defragmentation:** ~1-100 ms (Mutex held through remap + event recording + GPU sync)\n- **Contended:** Adds waiting time proportional to queue depth\n\n**Per-Thread Buffer Reuse WITHOUT Lock Bypass:**\n- ✅ Can reuse same-stream free regions efficiently (low sync cost)\n- ✅ Pool already optimizes for this (stream-affinity in `find_best_fit`)\n- ❌ **Cannot bypass Mutex** — required to read pool state\n- ❌ Per-thread TLS buffer cache would need synchronization with pool on eviction\n\n**Possible Optimizations (not currently implemented):**\n1. **Lock-free read path for current stream:** Use atomic snapshot of free regions (limited benefit)\n2. **Per-thread allocation arenas:** Thread-local pools that batch allocations (complexity trade-off)\n3. **Defragmentation preemption:** Yield lock if high contention detected (complex heuristics)\n4. **Async defragmentation:** Move remap to background task (requires careful event handling)"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"39e8685b-d752-4f1d-86a0-2956436bbd44","uuid":"8ac62217-7ec9-41e0-9707-2b04600878da","timestamp":"2026-04-13T03:38:48.761Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","promp