the batched kernel write to the right section. That mirrors how the scatter kernel already works. The implementation would involve creating a descriptor struct with per-AIR metadata, writing a new batched kernel that reads its descriptor per block, modifying the main function to build and upload these descriptors, and pre-computing the total intermediates size needed. I could also drop the multi-threading code since the batched kernel handles everything in one go.\n\nThe CUDA kernel itself is the trickiest part, but most of the existing logic can stay—I'd mainly add descriptor-based dispatch on top. Looking at the performance impact, consolidating 791 kernel launches into one saves roughly 4ms of launch overhead, and eliminating 1600 memory allocation/deallocation calls saves another 16ms. With all 128 SMs active throughout instead of partially utilized, kernel time should drop from 236ms to 60-120ms per segment. CPU overhead for descriptor building would be around 5ms per segment, putting wall time at 65-125ms instead of 267ms. Overall, the GKR input evaluation could go from 529ms down to 130-250ms—a savings of 280-400ms even in conservative estimates.\n\nThe real question is whether it's worth the risk. Success probability is maybe 50% given the kernel complexity, but even a partial win saves 100ms. Comparing expected values across options, the batched GKR kernel scores highest despite being the riskiest approach, so I'm leaning toward exploring a hybrid strategy that reduces risk without sacrificing too much upside.\n\nInstead of fully batching all AIRs together, I could batch only the small ones (height ≤ 128) while keeping the existing per-AIR kernel for large ones. At APC 300, that's roughly 500 small AIRs that could be packed into a single kernel with all SMs active, saving around 40-45ms in GPU time plus another 2ms from reduced launch overhead. It's not a massive gain, but the complexity drops significantly and the success probability improves.\n\nThe real bottleneck is the large AIRs (360ms total), which I initially thought couldn't be batched due to their varying interaction structures. But actually, the descriptor-based approach scales to them too—each large AIR just gets multiple blocks proportional to its height, and the kernel assigns work accordingly. With 120 large AIRs and 200-300 blocks per AIR, that's 24K-36K blocks total that the GPU can schedule efficiently across its SMs. The tricky part is handling the per-AIR buffers like `rules`, `used_nodes`, and `pair_idxs` which have different sizes and live on the GPU. The descriptor solves this by storing pointers to each AIR's buffers so the batched kernel knows where to find them.\n\nNow I'm convinced this is the right approach and need to plan the implementation. But I should double-check the constraints—the work needs to stay in the `stark-backend` repo, and the `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel` is already there in the CUDA code. The `apc_apply_bus_kernel` is in the powdr repo which we can't modify, but that's only used during trace generation, not STARK proving, so it shouldn't block this optimization.\n\nLooking at the other kernel launches in `process_gkr_input_air`, there are also `frac_matrix_vertically_repeat` and `frac_vector_scalar_multiply_ext_fp` calls that fire for each AIR when height-lifting is needed. Since many APCs at the 300 scale have skip values greater than zero, this creates additional overhead across multiple AIRs that could also be optimized.\n\nBefore I finalize the batching strategy, I should examine the `logup_gkr_input_eval` CUDA function itself to see how feasible it would be to consolidate these kernel launches.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6143,"cache_read_input_tokens":113379,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6143},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_avai