{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vx963mjaBGcQio1VAZ7Szu","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Pre-allocate per-thread GPU buffer pools for GKR input evaluation\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tEliminate per-AIR GPU memory allocation/deallocation inside the multi-threaded GKR input evaluation loop. Currently, each AIR in `process_gkr_input_air` allocates an `intermediates` DeviceBuffer and a `d_public_values` DeviceBuffer through the global `Mutex<MemoryManager>`, which also triggers `cudaMallocAsync`/`cudaFreeAsync` on the shared CUDA memory pool. With 8 worker threads, this creates two serialization points per allocation: (1) the Rust Mutex and (2) implicit CUDA pool cross-stream synchronization when reclaiming blocks. Pre-allocating these buffers before spawning threads and reusing them across AIRs should remove these serialization points and enable actual kernel concurrency on the 8 per-thread CUDA streams.\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\t### Entry point\n10\t\n11\t`log_gkr_input_evals()` at `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs:215-297`:\n12\t1. Allocates `leaves` buffer (line 228) — single allocation, not per-AIR\n13\t2. Builds work items from `trace_interactions`, sorts by descending height (lines 232-252)\n14\t3. Syncs main stream (line 255)\n15\t4. Spawns 8 OS threads via `std::thread::scope` (lines 272-291)\n16\t5. Each thread creates fresh `d_partition_ptrs` and `tmp` buffers (lines 277-278)\n17\t6. Each thread calls `process_gkr_input_air` sequentially for its chunk of AIRs\n18\t\n19\t### Per-AIR function\n20\t\n21\t`process_gkr_input_air()` at `gkr_input.rs:105-210`:\n22\t\n23\t**Allocations that go through MemoryManager per AIR:**\n24\t1. `d_public_values = air_ctx.public_values.to_device()` (line 135) — allocates `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(len)` + H2D copy. Only if `public_values` is non-empty.\n25\t2. `intermediates = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(TASK_SIZE * buffer_size)` (line 153) — when `buffer_size > 10` (i.e., `is_global = true`). For `is_global = false`, allocates capacity 1 (16 bytes, trivial).\n26\t3. `d_partition_ptrs` resize (line 145) — only if current capacity is too small. Already reused across AIRs.\n27\t4. `tmp` resize (line 167) — only if current capacity is too small AND `height != lifted_height`. Already reused across AIRs.\n28\t\n29\t**Deallocations per AIR (via Drop):**\n30\t- `intermediates` drops at end of `process_gkr_input_air` → `d_free()` → `Mutex<MemoryManager>::lock()`\n31\t- `d_public_values` drops at end of `process_gkr_input_air` → `d_free()` → `Mutex<MemoryManager>::lock()`\n32\t\n33\t### Lock contention path\n34\t\n35\t`d_malloc` at `crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/mod.rs:141-145`:\n36\t```rust\n37\tpub fn d_malloc(size: usize) -> Result<*mut c_void, MemoryError> {\n38\t    let manager = MEMORY_MANAGER.get().unwrap();\n39\t    let mut manager = manager.lock().map_err(|_| MemoryError::LockError)?;\n40\t    manager.d_malloc(size)  // Lock held for entire operation\n41\t}\n42\t```\n43\t\n44\tFor small allocations (< 2MB page_size): calls `cudaMallocAsync(stream)` while holding the Rust Mutex.\n45\tFor large allocations (>= 2MB): uses VirtualMemoryPool with potential defragmentation while holding the Mutex.\n46\t\n47\t`d_free` at `mod.rs:150-154` has the same lock pattern.\n48\t\n49\t### Why multi-streaming doesn't help\n50\t\n51\tWith 8 threads, each thread processes ~38 AIRs per segment (310 AIRs / 8). Per AIR, there are ~2 alloc + ~2 free calls through the Mutex. Total: ~310 × 4 = ~1240 Mutex acquisitions per segment under 8-way contention.\n52\t\n53\tAdditionally, `cudaMallocAsync` on per-thread streams shares a global CUDA memory pool. When one stream allocates, the CUDA runtime may synchronize with other streams to reclaim freed blocks, preventing true kernel concurrency.\n54\t\n55\tEvidence: GKR input eval kernel time = 471ms (across all instances). Wall time = 529ms across 2 segments (267ms + 262ms per segment). Per-segment: ~236ms GPU time in ~267ms wall time = effectively sequential (1.0x parallelism) despite 8 threads. Compare with Round 0 where the same architecture achieves 1.62x — Round 0 has fewer, larger allocations per AIR.\n56\t\n57\t## Changes\n58\t\n59\t### Change 1: Compute max buffer sizes before spawning threads\n60\t\n61\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n62\t**Function**: `log_gkr_input_evals`\n63\t**Where**: After building work items (line 250), before the sync barrier (line 255)\n64\t\n65\tAdd a pre-computation loop over all work items that determines:\n66\t- `max_intermediates_cap`: the maximum `TASK_SIZE * buffer_size` across all AIRs where `is_global = true` (buffer_size > 10). If no AIR is global, default to 1.\n67\t- `max_public_values_len`: the maximum `air_ctx.public_values.len()` across all AIRs.\n68\t- `max_partition_ptrs_len`: the maximum number of partitioned main traces across all AIRs.\n69\t- `max_tmp_cap`: the maximum `height * num_interactions` across all AIRs where `height != lifted_height`.\n70\t\n71\tThis is pure CPU work on data already available in the work items — no GPU calls needed.\n72\t\n73\t### Change 2: Pre-allocate per-thread buffer pools\n74\t\n75\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n76\t**Function**: `log_gkr_input_evals`\n77\t**Where**: After the sync barrier (line 255), before spawning threads (line 272)\n78\t\n79\tCreate a new struct:\n80\t```rust\n81\tstruct GkrThreadBuffers {\n82\t    intermediates: DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n83\t    public_values: DeviceBuffer<F>,\n84\t    partition_ptrs: DeviceBuffer<u64>,\n85\t    tmp: DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>,\n86\t}\n87\t```\n88\t\n89\tAllocate `num_threads` instances of `GkrThreadBuffers`, each with:\n90\t- `intermediates`: `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(max_intermediates_cap)`\n91\t- `public_values`: `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(max_public_values_len)`\n92\t- `partition_ptrs`: `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(max_partition_ptrs_len)`\n93\t- `tmp`: `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(max_tmp_cap)`\n94\t\n95\tThese allocations happen on the main thread's CUDA stream BEFORE any worker thread is spawned. Since the main stream is synced at line 255, all buffers are fully allocated before workers start.\n96\t\n97\t### Change 3: Pass pre-allocated buffers to worker threads\n98\t\n99\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n100\t**Function**: `log_gkr_input_evals`\n101\t\n102\tModify the thread-spawning code (lines 272-291) to pass one `GkrThreadBuffers` to each thread instead of creating fresh buffers inside each thread. Use `into_iter().zip(chunks)` to pair each buffer set with its chunk of work items.\n103\t\n104\tFor the single-thread path (lines 264-269), use one buffer set directly.\n105\t\n106\t### Change 4: Modify `process_gkr_input_air` to accept pre-allocated buffers\n107\t\n108\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n109\t**Function**: `process_gkr_input_air`\n110\t\n111\tChange the function signature from:\n112\t```rust\n113\tfn process_gkr_input_air<HS: GpuHashScheme>(\n114\t    w: &GkrInputWorkItem<HS>,\n115\t    d_partition_ptrs: &mut DeviceBuffer<u64>,\n116\t    tmp: &mut DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>,\n117\t) -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError>\n118\t```\n119\t\n120\tTo:\n121\t```rust\n122\tfn process_gkr_input_air<HS: GpuHashScheme>(\n123\t    w: &GkrInputWorkItem<HS>,\n124\t    buffers: &mut GkrThreadBuffers,\n125\t) -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError>\n126\t```\n127\t\n128\tInside the function, replace allocations:\n129\t\n130\t1. **`intermediates`** (lines 152-156): Instead of allocating, use `&buffers.intermediates`. The pre-allocated buffer is already at least `TASK_SIZE * buffer_size` capacity. For `is_global = false`, the intermediates buffer still exists (capacity 1 or more) — the kernel ignores it since `is_global = false` uses stack-local intermediates.\n131\t\n132\t2. **`d_public_values`** (lines 132-136): Instead of `to_device()`, do a `cudaMemcpyAsync` into `buffers.public_values` (the pre-allocated buffer). Use a helper like `copy_to_preallocated(&buffers.public_values)` that does H2D copy without allocating a new buffer. If `public_values` is empty, pass the empty buffer pointer directly (same as current `DeviceBuffer::new()`).\n133\t\n134\t3. **`d_partition_ptrs`** (lines 144-147): Use `buffers.partition_ptrs` directly instead of the old `d_partition_ptrs` parameter. The capacity check (line 144) is no longer needed since it's pre-allocated to max size.\n135\t\n136\t4. **`tmp`** (lines 164-172): Use `buffers.tmp` directly. The capacity check (line 166) is no longer needed.\n137\t\n138\t### Change 5: Add `copy_into` helper for H2D copy to pre-allocated buffer\n139\t\n140\t**File**: `crates/cuda-common/src/copy.rs` (or inline in gkr_input.rs)\n141\t\n142\tAdd a function that copies a host slice into an existing DeviceBuffer without reallocation:\n143\t```rust\n144\tfn copy_into_device_buffer<T: Copy>(src: &[T], dst: &mut DeviceBuffer<T>) -> Result<(), CudaError> {\n145\t    debug_assert!(src.len() <= dst.capacity());\n146\t    unsafe {\n147\t        cudaMemcpyAsync(\n148\t            dst.as_mut_ptr() as *mut c_void,\n149\t            src.as_ptr() as *const c_void,\n150\t            src.len() * std::mem::size_of::<T>(),\n151\t            cudaMemcpyHostToDevice,\n152\t            cudaStreamPerThread,\n153\t        );\n154\t    }\n155\t    Ok(())\n156\t}\n157\t```\n158\t\n159\tThis avoids the existing `to_device()` path that allocates a new DeviceBuffer.\n160\t\n161\tNote: The pre-allocated buffer's `len()` tracking is not updated (it still reports the capacity). This is fine because the kernel uses explicit `height` / `used_nodes_len` parameters to determine how much data to read, not the buffer's reported length. The same pattern is already used for `d_partition_ptrs` in the current code (line 147: `partition_ptrs.copy_to(d_partition_ptrs)` overwrites content without adjusting capacity).\n162\t\n163\t### Change 6: Handle the single-threaded path consistently\n164\t\n165\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n166\t\n167\tFor `num_threads <= 1` (lines 264-269): create one `GkrThreadBuffers` with the same pre-computed max sizes, and pass it to the sequential loop.\n168\t\n169\t## Invariants\n170\t\n171\t1. **Correctness**: Every kernel call receives identical pointer arguments and sizes as before. The only change is that buffers are pre-allocated rather than per-AIR allocated. The buffer content is overwritten before each kernel launch (H2D copy for `partition_ptrs` and `public_values`, kernel writes for `intermediates`), so stale data from previous AIRs does not affect correctness.\n172\t\n173\t2. **Buffer capacity**: Pre-allocated buffers are sized to the maximum across ALL AIRs in the segment. Every AIR's requirements fit within the pre-allocated capacity. This is guaranteed by the pre-computation in Change 1.\n174\t\n175\t3. **Stream ordering**: Each thread's kernels execute sequentially on its per-thread CUDA stream (`cudaStreamPerThread`). Buffer reuse across AIRs within the same thread is safe because the CUDA stream guarantees that kernel N finishes before kernel N+1 reads the buffer. No explicit synchronization is needed between uses within a thread.\n176\t\n177\t4. **Cross-thread safety**: Each thread has its own `GkrThreadBuffers` instance with its own GPU memory. No two threads share a buffer. The `SendPtr` pattern already used for `leaves_ptr` ensures the output writes to non-overlapping regions.\n178\t\n179\t5. **APC 0 path**: When `work_items.len() < 100` (APC 0 with ~20 AIRs per segment), the single-threaded path is used. Pre-allocation still occurs but with only 1 buffer set. This should have negligible overhead.\n180\t\n181\t6. **Memory budget**: The pre-allocated buffers use at most `num_threads * max_intermediates_cap * sizeof(EF)` bytes. With 8 threads and a max buffer_size of, say, 500: 8 × 65536 × 500 × 16 = ~4GB. If this exceeds available GPU memory, fall back to per-AIR allocation for threads with large AIRs. However, in practice, only ~10-20 AIRs have large `buffer_size` (>10), and their intermediates are typically < 100MB each. The max across all AIRs determines the per-thread allocation, so worst case is 8 × max_single_AIR_intermediates.\n182\t\n183\t   **Safety valve**: If `max_intermediates_cap * sizeof(EF) * num_threads` exceeds a configurable limit (e.g., 2GB), reduce `num_threads` or fall back to per-AIR allocation. This prevents OOM on GPUs with less memory.\n184\t\n185\t## Measurement Plan\n186\t\n187\t### Before changes\n188\tRun `run_pairing.sh` for APC {0, 100, 300}. Record:\n189\t- `prover.rap_constraints.logup_gkr.input_evals_time_ms` per segment\n190\t- `prover.rap_constraints.logup_gkr_time_ms` per segment\n191\t- `stark_prove_excluding_trace_time_ms` per segment\n192\t- Full `spec.py` output for all three configs\n193\t\n194\t### After changes\n195\tRun the same benchmarks. Compare:\n196\t\n197\t**Primary metric**: `prover.rap_constraints.logup_gkr.input_evals_time_ms` (sum across segments)\n198\t- Target: < 400ms at APC 300 (vs current ~529ms, requiring >25% improvement)\n199\t\n200\t**Secondary metric**: `stark_prove_excluding_trace_time_ms` (spec.py \"STARK excl trace\")\n201\t- Target: < 1600ms at APC 300 (vs current ~1720ms, requiring >7% improvement)\n202\t\n203\t**No-regression**: STARK excl trace at APC 0 must not increase by more than 3% (< 2200ms).\n204\t\n205\t### Nsight validation\n206\tRun nsight on APC 300. Compare kernel overlap:\n207\t- Before: GKR input eval kernels on different streams should show minimal overlap\n208\t- After: GKR input eval kernels on different streams should show concurrent execution\n209\t\n210\t### Correctness\n211\tAll three APC configs must complete prove+verify successfully.\n212\t\n213\t## Rollback Criteria\n214\t\n215\tRevert if ANY of:\n216\t1. STARK excl trace APC 300 improvement < 60ms (< 3.5% improvement)\n217\t2. STARK excl trace APC 0 regresses by > 65ms\n218\t3. GPU OOM at any APC config\n219\t4. Prove+verify fails at any config\n220\t5. GKR input eval wall time does not improve by at least 15% at APC 300\n221\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"39e8685b-d752-4f1d-86a0-2956436bbd44","uuid":"127be01b-4605-4e78-88f9-b910fd861723","timestamp":"2026-04-13T04:03:09.361Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-13-2100-prealloc-gkr-input-buffers/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Pre-allocate per-thread GPU buffer pools for GKR input evaluation\n\n## Goal\n\nEliminate per-AIR GPU memory allocation/deallocation inside the multi-threaded GKR input evaluation loop. Currently, each AIR in `process_gkr_input_air` allocates an `intermediates` DeviceBuffer and a `d_public_values` DeviceBuffer through the global `Mutex<MemoryManager>`, which also triggers `cudaMallocAsync`/`cudaFreeAsync` on the shared CUDA memory pool. With 8 worker threads, this creates two serialization points per allocation: (1) the Rust Mutex and (2) implicit CUDA pool cross-stream synchronization when reclaiming blocks. Pre-allocating these buffers before spawning threads and reusing them across AIRs should remove these serialization points and enable actual kernel concurrency on the 8 per-thread CUDA streams.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Entry point\n\n`log_gkr_input_evals()` at `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs:215-297`:\n1. Allocates `leaves` buffer (line 228) — single allocation, not per-AIR\n2. Builds work items from `trace_interactions`, sorts by descending height (lines 232-252)\n3. Syncs main stream (line 255)\n4. Spawns 8 OS threads via `std::thread::scope` (lines 272-291)\n5. Each thread creates fresh `d_partition_ptrs` and `tmp` buffers (lines 277-278)\n6. Each thread calls `process_gkr_input_air` sequentially for its chunk of AIRs\n\n### Per-AIR function\n\n`process_gkr_input_air()` at `gkr_input.rs:105-210`:\n\n**Allocations that go through MemoryManager per AIR:**\n1. `d_public_values = air_ctx.public_values.to_device()` (line 135) — allocates `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(len)` + H2D copy. Only if `public_values` is non-empty.\n2. `intermediates = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(TASK_SIZE * buffer_size)` (line 153) — when `buffer_size > 10` (i.e., `is_global = true`). For `is_global = false`, allocates capacity 1 (16 bytes, trivial).\n3. `d_partition_ptrs` resize (line 145) — only if current capacity is too small. Already reused across AIRs.\n4. `tmp` resize (line 167) — only if current capacity is too small AND `height != lifted_height`. Already reused across AIRs.\n\n**Deallocations per AIR (via Drop):**\n- `intermediates` drops at end of `process_gkr_input_air` → `d_free()` → `Mutex<MemoryManager>::lock()`\n- `d_public_values` drops at end of `process_gkr_input_air` → `d_free()` → `Mutex<MemoryManager>::lock()`\n\n### Lock contention path\n\n`d_malloc` at `crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/mod.rs:141-145`:\n```rust\npub fn d_malloc(size: usize) -> Result<*mut c_void, MemoryError> {\n    let manager = MEMORY_MANAGER.get().unwrap();\n    let mut manager = manager.lock().map_err(|_| MemoryError::LockError)?;\n    manager.d_malloc(size)  // Lock held for entire operation\n}\n```\n\nFor small allocations (< 2MB page_size): calls `cudaMallocAsync(stream)` while holding the Rust Mutex.\nFor large allocations (>= 2MB): uses VirtualMemoryPool with potential defragmentation while holding the Mutex.\n\n`d_free` at `mod.rs:150-154` has the same lock pattern.\n\n### Why multi-streaming doesn't help\n\nWith 8 threads, each thread processes ~38 AIRs per segment (310 AIRs / 8). Per AIR, there are ~2 alloc + ~2 free calls through the Mutex. Total: ~310 × 4 = ~1240 Mutex acquisitions per segment under 8-way contention.\n\nAdditionally, `cudaMallocAsync` on per-thread streams shares a global CUDA memory pool. When one stream allocates, the CUDA runtime may synchronize with other streams to reclaim freed blocks, preventing true kernel concurrency.\n\nEvidence: GKR input eval kernel time = 471ms (across all instances). Wall time = 529ms across 2 segments (267ms + 262ms per segment). Per-segment: ~236ms GPU time in ~267ms wall time = effectively sequential (1.0x parallelism) despite 8 threads. Compare with Round 0 where the same architecture achieves 1.62x — Round 0 has fewer, larger allocations per AIR.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: Compute max buffer sizes before spawning threads\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n**Function**: `log_gkr_input_evals`\n**Where**: After building work items (line 250), before the sync barrier (line 255)\n\nAdd a pre-computation loop over all work items that determines:\n- `max_intermediates_cap`: the maximum `TASK_SIZE * buffer_size` across all AIRs where `is_global = true` (buffer_size > 10). If no AIR is global, default to 1.\n- `max_public_values_len`: the maximum `air_ctx.public_values.len()` across all AIRs.\n- `max_partition_ptrs_len`: the maximum number of partitioned main traces across all AIRs.\n- `max_tmp_cap`: the maximum `height * num_interactions` across all AIRs where `height != lifted_height`.\n\nThis is pure CPU work on data already available in the work items — no GPU calls needed.\n\n### Change 2: Pre-allocate per-thread buffer pools\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n**Function**: `log_gkr_input_evals`\n**Where**: After the sync barrier (line 255), before spawning threads (line 272)\n\nCreate a new struct:\n```rust\nstruct GkrThreadBuffers {\n    intermediates: DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n    public_values: DeviceBuffer<F>,\n    partition_ptrs: DeviceBuffer<u64>,\n    tmp: DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>,\n}\n```\n\nAllocate `num_threads` instances of `GkrThreadBuffers`, each with:\n- `intermediates`: `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(max_intermediates_cap)`\n- `public_values`: `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(max_public_values_len)`\n- `partition_ptrs`: `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(max_partition_ptrs_len)`\n- `tmp`: `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(max_tmp_cap)`\n\nThese allocations happen on the main thread's CUDA stream BEFORE any worker thread is spawned. Since the main stream is synced at line 255, all buffers are fully allocated before workers start.\n\n### Change 3: Pass pre-allocated buffers to worker threads\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n**Function**: `log_gkr_input_evals`\n\nModify the thread-spawning code (lines 272-291) to pass one `GkrThreadBuffers` to each thread instead of creating fresh buffers inside each thread. Use `into_iter().zip(chunks)` to pair each buffer set with its chunk of work items.\n\nFor the single-thread path (lines 264-269), use one buffer set directly.\n\n### Change 4: Modify `process_gkr_input_air` to accept pre-allocated buffers\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n**Function**: `process_gkr_input_air`\n\nChange the function signature from:\n```rust\nfn process_gkr_input_air<HS: GpuHashScheme>(\n    w: &GkrInputWorkItem<HS>,\n    d_partition_ptrs: &mut DeviceBuffer<u64>,\n    tmp: &mut DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>,\n) -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError>\n```\n\nTo:\n```rust\nfn process_gkr_input_air<HS: GpuHashScheme>(\n    w: &GkrInputWorkItem<HS>,\n    buffers: &mut GkrThreadBuffers,\n) -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError>\n```\n\nInside the function, replace allocations:\n\n1. **`intermediates`** (lines 152-156): Instead of allocating, use `&buffers.intermediates`. The pre-allocated buffer is already at least `TASK_SIZE * buffer_size` capacity. For `is_global = false`, the intermediates buffer still exists (capacity 1 or more) — the kernel ignores it since `is_global = false` uses stack-local intermediates.\n\n2. **`d_public_values`** (lines 132-136): Instead of `to_device()`, do a `cudaMemcpyAsync` into `buffers.public_values` (the pre-allocated buffer). Use a helper like `copy_to_preallocated(&buffers.public_values)` that does H2D copy without allocating a new buffer. If `public_values` is empty, pass the empty buffer pointer directly (same as current `DeviceBuffer::new()`).\n\n3. **`d_partition_ptrs`** (lines 144-147): Use `buffers.partition_ptrs` directly instead of the old `d_partition_ptrs` parameter. The capacity check (line 144) is no longer needed since it's pre-allocated to max size.\n\n4. **`tmp`** (lines 164-172): Use `buffers.tmp` directly. The capacity check (line 166) is no longer needed.\n\n### Change 5: Add `copy_into` helper for H2D copy to pre-allocated buffer\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-common/src/copy.rs` (or inline in gkr_input.rs)\n\nAdd a function that copies a host slice into an existing DeviceBuffer without reallocation:\n```rust\nfn copy_into_device_buffer<T: Copy>(src: &[T], dst: &mut DeviceBuffer<T>) -> Result<(), CudaError> {\n    debug_assert!(src.len() <= dst.capacity());\n    unsafe {\n        cudaMemcpyAsync(\n            dst.as_mut_ptr() as *mut c_void,\n            src.as_ptr() as *const c_void,\n            src.len() * std::mem::size_of::<T>(),\n            cudaMemcpyHostToDevice,\n            cudaStreamPerThread,\n        );\n    }\n    Ok(())\n}\n```\n\nThis avoids the existing `to_device()` path that allocates a new DeviceBuffer.\n\nNote: The pre-allocated buffer's `len()` tracking is not updated (it still reports the capacity). This is fine because the kernel uses explicit `height` / `used_nodes_len` parameters to determine how much data to read, not the buffer's reported length. The same pattern is already used for `d_partition_ptrs` in the current code (line 147: `partition_ptrs.copy_to(d_partition_ptrs)` overwrites content without adjusting capacity).\n\n### Change 6: Handle the single-threaded path consistently\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n\nFor `num_threads <= 1` (lines 264-269): create one `GkrThreadBuffers` with the same pre-computed max sizes, and pass it to the sequential loop.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Correctness**: Every kernel call receives identical pointer arguments and sizes as before. The only change is that buffers are pre-allocated rather than per-AIR allocated. The buffer content is overwritten before each kernel launch (H2D copy for `partition_ptrs` and `public_values`, kernel writes for `intermediates`), so stale data from previous AIRs does not affect correctness.\n\n2. **Buffer capacity**: Pre-allocated buffers are sized to the maximum across ALL AIRs in the segment. Every AIR's requirements fit within the pre-allocated capacity. This is guaranteed by the pre-computation in Change 1.\n\n3. **Stream ordering**: Each thread's kernels execute sequentially on its per-thread CUDA stream (`cudaStreamPerThread`). Buffer reuse across AIRs within the same thread is safe because the CUDA stream guarantees that kernel N finishes before kernel N+1 reads the buffer. No explicit synchronization is needed between uses within a thread.\n\n4. **Cross-thread safety**: Each thread has its own `GkrThreadBuffers` instance with its own GPU memory. No two threads share a buffer. The `SendPtr` pattern already used for `leaves_ptr` ensures the output writes to non-overlapping regions.\n\n5. **APC 0 path**: When `work_items.len() < 100` (APC 0 with ~20 AIRs per segment), the single-threaded path is used. Pre-allocation still occurs but with only 1 buffer set. This should have negligible overhead.\n\n6. **Memory budget**: The pre-allocated buffers use at most `num_threads * max_intermediates_cap * sizeof(EF)` bytes. With 8 threads and a max buffer_size of, say, 500: 8 × 65536 × 500 × 16 = ~4GB. If this exceeds available GPU memory, fall back to per-AIR allocation for threads with large AIRs. However, in practice, only ~10-20 AIRs have large `buffer_size` (>10), and their intermediates are typically < 100MB each. The max across all AIRs determines the per-thread allocation, so worst case is 8 × max_single_AIR_intermediates.\n\n   **Safety valve**: If `max_intermediates_cap * sizeof(EF) * num_threads` exceeds a configurable limit (e.g., 2GB), reduce `num_threads` or fall back to per-AIR allocation. This prevents OOM on GPUs with less memory.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n### Before changes\nRun `run_pairing.sh` for APC {0, 100, 300}. Record:\n- `prover.rap_constraints.logup_gkr.input_evals_time_ms` per segment\n- `prover.rap_constraints.logup_gkr_time_ms` per segment\n- `stark_prove_excluding_trace_time_ms` per segment\n- Full `spec.py` output for all three configs\n\n### After changes\nRun the same benchmarks. Compare:\n\n**Primary metric**: `prover.rap_constraints.logup_gkr.input_evals_time_ms` (sum across segments)\n- Target: < 400ms at APC 300 (vs current ~529ms, requiring >25% improvement)\n\n**Secondary metric**: `stark_prove_excluding_trace_time_ms` (spec.py \"STARK excl trace\")\n- Target: < 1600ms at APC 300 (vs current ~1720ms, requiring >7% improvement)\n\n**No-regression**: STARK excl trace at APC 0 must not increase by more than 3% (< 2200ms).\n\n### Nsight validation\nRun nsight on APC 300. Compare kernel overlap:\n- Before: GKR input eval kernels on different streams should show minimal overlap\n- After: GKR input eval kernels on different streams should show concurrent execution\n\n### Correctness\nAll three APC configs must complete prove+verify successfully.\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\nRevert if ANY of:\n1. STARK excl trace APC 300 improvement < 60ms (< 3.5% improvement)\n2. STARK excl trace APC 0 regresses by > 65ms\n3. GPU OOM at any APC config\n4. Prove+verify fails at any config\n5. GKR input eval wall time does not improve by at least 15% at APC 300\n","numLines":221,"startLine":1,"totalLines":221}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BkXDx5DgqW8CLskCV3e2Y1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014DCRbQuGbisfvbVzGQ7dn8","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-13-2100-prealloc-gkr-input-buffers/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Pre-allocate per-thread GPU buffer pools for GKR input evaluation\n\n## Goal\n\nEliminate per-AIR GPU memory allocation/deallocation inside the multi-threaded GKR input evaluation loop. Currently, each AIR in `process_gkr_input_air` allocates an `intermediates` DeviceBuffer and a `d_public_values` DeviceBuffer through the global `Mutex<MemoryManager>`, which also triggers `cudaMallocAsync`/`cudaFreeAsync` on the shared CUDA memory pool. With 8 worker threads, this creates two serialization points per allocation: (1) the Rust Mutex and (2) implicit CUDA pool cross-stream synchronization when reclaiming blocks. Pre-allocating these buffers before spawning threads and reusing them across AIRs should remove these serialization points and enable actual kernel concurrency on the 8 per-thread CUDA streams.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Entry point\n\n`log_gkr_input_evals()` at `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs:215-297`:\n1. Allocates `leaves` buffer (line 228) — single allocation, not per-AIR\n2. Builds work items from `trace_interactions`, sorts by descending height (lines 232-252)\n3. Syncs main stream (line 255)\n4. Spawns 8 OS threads via `std::thread::scope` (lines 272-291)\n5. Each thread creates fresh `d_partition_ptrs` and `tmp` buffers (lines 277-278)\n6. Each thread calls `process_gkr_input_air` sequentially for its chunk of AIRs\n\n### Per-AIR function\n\n`process_gkr_input_air()` at `gkr_input.rs:105-210`:\n\n**Allocations that go through MemoryManager per AIR:**\n1. `d_public_values = air_ctx.public_values.to_device()` (line 135) — calls `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(len)` + H2D copy. Only when `public_values` is non-empty.\n2. `intermediates = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(TASK_SIZE * buffer_size)` (line 153) — when `buffer_size > 10` (i.e., `is_global = true`). For `is_global = false`, allocates with length 1 (16 bytes, trivial but still takes the Mutex).\n3. `d_partition_ptrs` resize (line 145) — only if current length is too small. Already reused across AIRs within a thread.\n4. `tmp` resize (line 167) — only if current length is too small AND `height != lifted_height`. Already reused across AIRs within a thread.\n\n**Deallocations per AIR (via Drop):**\n- `intermediates` drops at end of `process_gkr_input_air` → `d_free()` → `Mutex<MemoryManager>::lock()`\n- `d_public_values` drops at end of `process_gkr_input_air` → `d_free()` → `Mutex<MemoryManager>::lock()`\n\nNote: `DeviceBuffer` has no separate capacity/length distinction. `with_capacity(n)` allocates and sets `len = n` (`d_buffer.rs:60-82`). The `len()` method returns this allocated length.\n\n### Lock contention path\n\n`d_malloc` at `crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/mod.rs:141-145`:\n```rust\npub fn d_malloc(size: usize) -> Result<*mut c_void, MemoryError> {\n    let manager = MEMORY_MANAGER.get().unwrap();\n    let mut manager = manager.lock().map_err(|_| MemoryError::LockError)?;\n    manager.d_malloc(size)  // Lock held for entire operation\n}\n```\n\nFor small allocations (< 2MB page_size): calls `cudaMallocAsync(stream)` while holding the Rust Mutex.\nFor large allocations (>= 2MB): uses VirtualMemoryPool with potential defragmentation while holding the Mutex.\n\n`d_free` at `mod.rs:150-154` has the same lock pattern.\n\n### Why multi-streaming doesn't help\n\nWith 8 threads, each thread processes ~38 AIRs per segment (310 AIRs / 8). Per AIR, there are ~2 alloc + ~2 free calls through the Mutex. Total: ~310 × 4 = ~1240 Mutex acquisitions per segment under 8-way contention.\n\nAdditionally, `cudaMallocAsync` on per-thread streams shares a global CUDA memory pool. When one stream allocates, the CUDA runtime may synchronize with other streams to reclaim freed blocks, preventing true kernel concurrency.\n\nEvidence: GKR input eval GPU kernel time = 471ms total (sum of all `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel` instances across both segments). Wall time = 529ms (267ms + 262ms per segment). Per-segment: ~236ms GPU kernel time in ~267ms wall time = effectively sequential (1.0x parallelism) despite 8 threads. Compare with Round 0 where the same multi-stream architecture achieves 1.62x — Round 0 has fewer per-AIR allocations (reuses `intermediates` via the `max_temp_bytes` budget rather than allocating per-AIR).\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: Compute max buffer sizes before spawning threads\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n**Function**: `log_gkr_input_evals`\n**Where**: After building and sorting work items (after line 252), before the sync barrier (line 255)\n\nAdd a pre-computation loop over all work items:\n\n```rust\nlet mut max_intermediates_len: usize = 1;\nlet mut max_public_values_len: usize = 0;\nlet mut max_partition_ptrs_len: usize = 0;\nlet mut max_tmp_len: usize = 0;\n\nfor w in &work_items {\n    let rules = &w.pk_air.other_data.interaction_rules;\n    let buffer_size = rules.inner.buffer_size as usize;\n    let is_global = buffer_size > 10;\n    if is_global {\n        max_intermediates_len = max_intermediates_len.max(TASK_SIZE as usize * buffer_size);\n    }\n\n    max_public_values_len = max_public_values_len.max(w.air_ctx.public_values.len());\n\n    let num_partitions = w.air_ctx.cached_mains.len() + 1;\n    max_partition_ptrs_len = max_partition_ptrs_len.max(num_partitions);\n\n    let height = w.air_ctx.height();\n    let lifted_height = height.max(1 << w.l_skip);\n    if height != lifted_height {\n        let num_interactions = w.pk_air.vk.symbolic_constraints.interactions.len();\n        max_tmp_len = max_tmp_len.max(height * num_interactions);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis is pure CPU work on data already available in the work items — no GPU calls.\n\n### Change 2: Pre-allocate per-thread buffer pools\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n**Function**: `log_gkr_input_evals`\n**Where**: After the sync barrier (line 255), before spawning threads (line 272)\n\nDefine a struct to hold per-thread buffers:\n```rust\nstruct GkrThreadBuffers {\n    intermediates: DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n    public_values: DeviceBuffer<F>,\n    partition_ptrs: DeviceBuffer<u64>,\n    tmp: DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>,\n}\n```\n\nAllocate `num_threads` instances. For each buffer, use `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(max_len)` if the max is > 0, or `DeviceBuffer::new()` if 0:\n\n```rust\nlet thread_buffers: Vec<GkrThreadBuffers> = (0..num_threads)\n    .map(|_| GkrThreadBuffers {\n        intermediates: DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(max_intermediates_len),\n        public_values: if max_public_values_len > 0 {\n            DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(max_public_values_len)\n        } else {\n            DeviceBuffer::new()\n        },\n        partition_ptrs: DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(max_partition_ptrs_len),\n        tmp: if max_tmp_len > 0 {\n            DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(max_tmp_len)\n        } else {\n            DeviceBuffer::new()\n        },\n    })\n    .collect();\n```\n\nThese allocations happen on the main thread's CUDA stream. All `num_threads` buffer sets are allocated sequentially (no contention), before any worker thread is spawned.\n\n**Memory budget check**: Before allocating, compute `total_bytes = num_threads * (max_intermediates_len * 16 + max_public_values_len * 4 + max_partition_ptrs_len * 8 + max_tmp_len * 64)`. If this exceeds 2 GB, reduce `num_threads` (halving until it fits) to avoid OOM. Log a warning if reduction occurs. This handles the edge case where a few AIRs with very large `buffer_size` values make per-thread pre-allocation expensive.\n\n### Change 3: Pass pre-allocated buffers to worker threads\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n**Function**: `log_gkr_input_evals`\n\nModify the thread-spawning code (lines 272-291). Pair each `GkrThreadBuffers` with its chunk of work items using `into_iter().zip()`:\n\n```rust\nlet chunk_size = work_items.len().div_ceil(num_threads);\nstd::thread::scope(|s| {\n    let handles: Vec<_> = thread_buffers\n        .into_iter()\n        .zip(work_items.chunks(chunk_size))\n        .map(|(mut bufs, chunk)| {\n            s.spawn(move || -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError> {\n                for w in chunk {\n                    process_gkr_input_air(w, &mut bufs)?;\n                }\n                current_stream_sync().map_err(InteractionGpuError::from)?;\n                Ok(())\n            })\n        })\n        .collect();\n    // ... join handles as before\n});\n```\n\nFor the single-thread path (`num_threads <= 1`): use `thread_buffers.into_iter().next().unwrap()` and pass to the sequential loop.\n\n### Change 4: Modify `process_gkr_input_air` to use pre-allocated buffers\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n**Function**: `process_gkr_input_air`\n\nChange the function signature:\n```rust\nfn process_gkr_input_air<HS: GpuHashScheme>(\n    w: &GkrInputWorkItem<HS>,\n    buffers: &mut GkrThreadBuffers,\n) -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError>\n```\n\nReplace per-AIR allocations:\n\n1. **`intermediates`** (lines 152-156): Remove the `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(...)` allocation. Use `&buffers.intermediates` directly. The pre-allocated buffer has `len >= TASK_SIZE * buffer_size` for all AIRs. For `is_global = false`, the kernel uses thread-local stack intermediates and ignores the global pointer, so the pre-allocated buffer is unused but harmless.\n\n2. **`d_public_values`** (lines 132-136): Keep the `if air_ctx.public_values.is_empty()` conditional to produce `DeviceBuffer::new()` (null pointer) for AIRs with no public values — this preserves the null-pointer semantics the kernel may rely on. For non-empty public values, use `air_ctx.public_values.copy_to(&mut buffers.public_values)?` (the existing `MemCopyH2D::copy_to` at `copy.rs:68-87`) which does `cudaMemcpyAsync` into the pre-allocated buffer without reallocation. The check `self.len() > dst.len()` passes because the source length ≤ max_public_values_len = pre-allocated length. Then pass `&buffers.public_values` to the kernel.\n\n3. **`d_partition_ptrs`** (lines 140-147): Use `buffers.partition_ptrs` instead of the old `d_partition_ptrs` parameter. Remove the resize check at line 144 since pre-allocated size is the max. Use `partition_ptrs.copy_to(&mut buffers.partition_ptrs)?` as before.\n\n4. **`tmp`** (lines 164-172): Use `buffers.tmp` instead of the old `tmp` parameter. Remove the resize check at line 166. Note: `buffers.tmp.len()` equals `max_tmp_len` (the max across all AIRs), which may be larger than `height * num_interactions` for the current AIR. This is safe because:\n   - `frac_vector_scalar_multiply_ext_fp` (line 198) uses `tmp.len()` as the element count — processing extra elements is harmless (multiplies stale data by scalar, result is never read).\n   - `frac_matrix_vertically_repeat` (line 200) uses explicit `num_interactions`, `lifted_height`, `height` parameters to determine read/write bounds, not `tmp.len()`.\n   - However, to avoid unnecessary GPU work on stale elements, the `frac_vector_scalar_multiply_ext_fp` call should pass `(height * num_interactions) as u32` instead of `tmp.len() as u32`. This is a one-line change at line 198.\n\n### Change 5: Handle the single-threaded path consistently\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n\nFor `num_threads <= 1` (lines 264-269): use the first (only) `GkrThreadBuffers` from `thread_buffers`:\n\n```rust\nif num_threads <= 1 {\n    let mut bufs = thread_buffers.into_iter().next().unwrap();\n    for w in &work_items {\n        process_gkr_input_air(w, &mut bufs)?;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Correctness**: Every kernel call receives the same data as before. Buffers are overwritten before each kernel launch (`copy_to` for `partition_ptrs` and `public_values`, kernel writes for `intermediates`). Stale data from previous AIRs in pre-allocated buffers is always overwritten before use or ignored by the kernel.\n\n2. **Buffer sizing**: Pre-allocated buffers are sized to the maximum `len` across ALL AIRs in the segment. Every AIR's requirements fit. This is guaranteed by the pre-computation in Change 1.\n\n3. **Stream ordering**: Each thread's kernels execute sequentially on its per-thread CUDA stream (`cudaStreamPerThread`). Buffer reuse across AIRs within the same thread is safe because CUDA stream ordering guarantees kernel N finishes before kernel N+1 reads the buffer.\n\n4. **Cross-thread safety**: Each thread owns its own `GkrThreadBuffers` with its own GPU memory. No two threads share a buffer. The `SendPtr` pattern already used for `leaves_ptr` ensures output writes to non-overlapping regions.\n\n5. **Cross-stream allocation/free**: Buffers are allocated on the main thread's stream and freed on worker threads' streams (via `Drop` when the worker's closure exits, after `current_stream_sync()`). This is safe: the worker stream is synchronized before the buffer drops, and `d_free` tracks allocations by pointer, not by stream.\n\n6. **Public values null semantics**: AIRs with empty `public_values` still receive `DeviceBuffer::new()` (null pointer, len=0), preserving the current kernel's expectation.\n\n7. **APC 0 path**: When `work_items.len() < 100` (APC 0 with ~20 AIRs per segment), the single-threaded path is used with 1 buffer set. Pre-allocation overhead is negligible.\n\n8. **Memory budget**: If `total pre-allocation > 2GB`, the code reduces `num_threads` (halving) until the budget fits. In the worst case, falls back to 1 thread. Logged as a warning. The RTX 4090 has 24GB; typical max intermediates per thread is < 100MB, so 8 × 100MB = 800MB is well within budget.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n### Before changes\nRun `run_pairing.sh` for APC {0, 100, 300}. Record:\n- `prover.rap_constraints.logup_gkr.input_evals_time_ms` per segment (from metrics JSON)\n- `prover.rap_constraints.logup_gkr_time_ms` per segment\n- `stark_prove_excluding_trace_time_ms` per segment\n- Full `spec.py` output for all three configs\n\n### After changes\nRun the same benchmarks. Compare:\n\n**Primary metric**: `prover.rap_constraints.logup_gkr.input_evals_time_ms` (sum across segments)\n- Target: < 400ms at APC 300 (vs current ~529ms, requiring >25% improvement)\n\n**Secondary metric**: `stark_prove_excluding_trace_time_ms` (spec.py \"STARK excl trace\")\n- Target: < 1600ms at APC 300 (vs current ~1720ms, requiring >7% improvement)\n\n**No-regression**: STARK excl trace at APC 0 must not increase by more than 3% (< 2200ms).\n\n### Nsight validation\nRun nsight on APC 300. Compare kernel concurrency:\n- Before: `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel` instances on different streams show minimal temporal overlap\n- After: instances on different streams show concurrent execution (visible in nsight timeline)\n\n### Correctness\nAll three APC configs must complete prove+verify successfully.\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\nRevert if ANY of:\n1. STARK excl trace APC 300 improvement < 60ms (< 3.5% improvement)\n2. STARK excl trace APC 0 regresses by > 65ms\n3. GPU OOM at any APC config\n4. Prove+verify fails at any config\n5. GKR input eval wall time does not improve by at least 15% at APC 300\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5390,"cache_read_input_tokens":162057,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5390},"output_tokens":38,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"39e8685b-d752-4f1d-86a0-2956436bbd44","uuid":"8682b88b-a8b7-47dd-afda-280c60441eab"}
