{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PdhMypU3dYfLX6vpQEnC8H","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Pre-allocate per-thread GPU buffer pools for GKR input evaluation\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tEliminate per-AIR GPU memory allocation/deallocation inside the multi-threaded GKR input evaluation loop. Currently, each AIR in `process_gkr_input_air` allocates an `intermediates` DeviceBuffer and a `d_public_values` DeviceBuffer through the global `Mutex<MemoryManager>`, which also triggers `cudaMallocAsync`/`cudaFreeAsync` on the shared CUDA memory pool. With 8 worker threads, this creates two serialization points per allocation: (1) the Rust Mutex and (2) implicit CUDA pool cross-stream synchronization when reclaiming blocks. Pre-allocating these buffers before spawning threads and reusing them across AIRs should remove these serialization points and enable actual kernel concurrency on the 8 per-thread CUDA streams.\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\t### Entry point\n10\t\n11\t`log_gkr_input_evals()` at `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs:215-297`:\n12\t1. Allocates `leaves` buffer (line 228) — single allocation, not per-AIR\n13\t2. Builds work items from `trace_interactions`, sorts by descending height (lines 232-252)\n14\t3. Syncs main stream (line 255)\n15\t4. Spawns 8 OS threads via `std::thread::scope` (lines 272-291)\n16\t5. Each thread creates fresh `d_partition_ptrs` and `tmp` buffers (lines 277-278)\n17\t6. Each thread calls `process_gkr_input_air` sequentially for its chunk of AIRs\n18\t\n19\t### Per-AIR function\n20\t\n21\t`process_gkr_input_air()` at `gkr_input.rs:105-210`:\n22\t\n23\t**Allocations that go through MemoryManager per AIR:**\n24\t1. `d_public_values = air_ctx.public_values.to_device()` (line 135) — calls `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(len)` + H2D copy. Only when `public_values` is non-empty.\n25\t2. `intermediates = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(TASK_SIZE * buffer_size)` (line 153) — when `buffer_size > 10` (i.e., `is_global = true`). For `is_global = false`, allocates with length 1 (16 bytes, trivial but still takes the Mutex).\n26\t3. `d_partition_ptrs` resize (line 145) — only if current length is too small. Already reused across AIRs within a thread.\n27\t4. `tmp` resize (line 167) — only if current length is too small AND `height != lifted_height`. Already reused across AIRs within a thread.\n28\t\n29\t**Deallocations per AIR (via Drop):**\n30\t- `intermediates` drops at end of `process_gkr_input_air` → `d_free()` → `Mutex<MemoryManager>::lock()`\n31\t- `d_public_values` drops at end of `process_gkr_input_air` → `d_free()` → `Mutex<MemoryManager>::lock()`\n32\t\n33\tNote: `DeviceBuffer` has no separate capacity/length distinction. `with_capacity(n)` allocates and sets `len = n` (`d_buffer.rs:60-82`). The `len()` method returns this allocated length.\n34\t\n35\t### Lock contention path\n36\t\n37\t`d_malloc` at `crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/mod.rs:141-145`:\n38\t```rust\n39\tpub fn d_malloc(size: usize) -> Result<*mut c_void, MemoryError> {\n40\t    let manager = MEMORY_MANAGER.get().unwrap();\n41\t    let mut manager = manager.lock().map_err(|_| MemoryError::LockError)?;\n42\t    manager.d_malloc(size)  // Lock held for entire operation\n43\t}\n44\t```\n45\t\n46\tFor small allocations (< 2MB page_size): calls `cudaMallocAsync(stream)` while holding the Rust Mutex.\n47\tFor large allocations (>= 2MB): uses VirtualMemoryPool with potential defragmentation while holding the Mutex.\n48\t\n49\t`d_free` at `mod.rs:150-154` has the same lock pattern.\n50\t\n51\t### Why multi-streaming doesn't help\n52\t\n53\tWith 8 threads, each thread processes ~38 AIRs per segment (310 AIRs / 8). Per AIR, there are ~2 alloc + ~2 free calls through the Mutex. Total: ~310 × 4 = ~1240 Mutex acquisitions per segment under 8-way contention.\n54\t\n55\tAdditionally, `cudaMallocAsync` on per-thread streams shares a global CUDA memory pool. When one stream allocates, the CUDA runtime may synchronize with other streams to reclaim freed blocks, preventing true kernel concurrency.\n56\t\n57\tEvidence: GKR input eval GPU kernel time = 471ms total (sum of all `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel` instances across both segments). Wall time = 529ms (267ms + 262ms per segment). Per-segment: ~236ms GPU kernel time in ~267ms wall time = effectively sequential (1.0x parallelism) despite 8 threads. Compare with Round 0 where the same multi-stream architecture achieves 1.62x — Round 0 has fewer per-AIR allocations (reuses `intermediates` via the `max_temp_bytes` budget rather than allocating per-AIR).\n58\t\n59\t## Changes\n60\t\n61\t### Change 1: Compute max buffer sizes before spawning threads\n62\t\n63\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n64\t**Function**: `log_gkr_input_evals`\n65\t**Where**: After building and sorting work items (after line 252), before the sync barrier (line 255)\n66\t\n67\tAdd a pre-computation loop over all work items:\n68\t\n69\t```rust\n70\tlet mut max_intermediates_len: usize = 1;\n71\tlet mut max_public_values_len: usize = 0;\n72\tlet mut max_partition_ptrs_len: usize = 0;\n73\tlet mut max_tmp_len: usize = 0;\n74\t\n75\tfor w in &work_items {\n76\t    let rules = &w.pk_air.other_data.interaction_rules;\n77\t    let buffer_size = rules.inner.buffer_size as usize;\n78\t    let is_global = buffer_size > 10;\n79\t    if is_global {\n80\t        max_intermediates_len = max_intermediates_len.max(TASK_SIZE as usize * buffer_size);\n81\t    }\n82\t\n83\t    max_public_values_len = max_public_values_len.max(w.air_ctx.public_values.len());\n84\t\n85\t    let num_partitions = w.air_ctx.cached_mains.len() + 1;\n86\t    max_partition_ptrs_len = max_partition_ptrs_len.max(num_partitions);\n87\t\n88\t    let height = w.air_ctx.height();\n89\t    let lifted_height = height.max(1 << w.l_skip);\n90\t    if height != lifted_height {\n91\t        let num_interactions = w.pk_air.vk.symbolic_constraints.interactions.len();\n92\t        max_tmp_len = max_tmp_len.max(height * num_interactions);\n93\t    }\n94\t}\n95\t```\n96\t\n97\tThis is pure CPU work on data already available in the work items — no GPU calls.\n98\t\n99\t### Change 2: Pre-allocate per-thread buffer pools\n100\t\n101\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n102\t**Function**: `log_gkr_input_evals`\n103\t**Where**: After the sync barrier (line 255), before spawning threads (line 272)\n104\t\n105\tDefine a struct to hold per-thread buffers:\n106\t```rust\n107\tstruct GkrThreadBuffers {\n108\t    intermediates: DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n109\t    public_values: DeviceBuffer<F>,\n110\t    partition_ptrs: DeviceBuffer<u64>,\n111\t    tmp: DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>,\n112\t}\n113\t```\n114\t\n115\tAllocate `num_threads` instances. For each buffer, use `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(max_len)` if the max is > 0, or `DeviceBuffer::new()` if 0:\n116\t\n117\t```rust\n118\tlet thread_buffers: Vec<GkrThreadBuffers> = (0..num_threads)\n119\t    .map(|_| GkrThreadBuffers {\n120\t        intermediates: DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(max_intermediates_len),\n121\t        public_values: if max_public_values_len > 0 {\n122\t            DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(max_public_values_len)\n123\t        } else {\n124\t            DeviceBuffer::new()\n125\t        },\n126\t        partition_ptrs: DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(max_partition_ptrs_len),\n127\t        tmp: if max_tmp_len > 0 {\n128\t            DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(max_tmp_len)\n129\t        } else {\n130\t            DeviceBuffer::new()\n131\t        },\n132\t    })\n133\t    .collect();\n134\t```\n135\t\n136\tThese allocations happen on the main thread's CUDA stream. All `num_threads` buffer sets are allocated sequentially (no contention), before any worker thread is spawned.\n137\t\n138\t**Memory budget check**: Before allocating, compute `total_bytes = num_threads * (max_intermediates_len * 16 + max_public_values_len * 4 + max_partition_ptrs_len * 8 + max_tmp_len * 64)`. If this exceeds 2 GB, reduce `num_threads` (halving until it fits) to avoid OOM. Log a warning if reduction occurs. This handles the edge case where a few AIRs with very large `buffer_size` values make per-thread pre-allocation expensive.\n139\t\n140\t### Change 3: Pass pre-allocated buffers to worker threads\n141\t\n142\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n143\t**Function**: `log_gkr_input_evals`\n144\t\n145\tModify the thread-spawning code (lines 272-291). Pair each `GkrThreadBuffers` with its chunk of work items using `into_iter().zip()`:\n146\t\n147\t```rust\n148\tlet chunk_size = work_items.len().div_ceil(num_threads);\n149\tstd::thread::scope(|s| {\n150\t    let handles: Vec<_> = thread_buffers\n151\t        .into_iter()\n152\t        .zip(work_items.chunks(chunk_size))\n153\t        .map(|(mut bufs, chunk)| {\n154\t            s.spawn(move || -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError> {\n155\t                for w in chunk {\n156\t                    process_gkr_input_air(w, &mut bufs)?;\n157\t                }\n158\t                current_stream_sync().map_err(InteractionGpuError::from)?;\n159\t                Ok(())\n160\t            })\n161\t        })\n162\t        .collect();\n163\t    // ... join handles as before\n164\t});\n165\t```\n166\t\n167\tFor the single-thread path (`num_threads <= 1`): use `thread_buffers.into_iter().next().unwrap()` and pass to the sequential loop.\n168\t\n169\t### Change 4: Modify `process_gkr_input_air` to use pre-allocated buffers\n170\t\n171\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n172\t**Function**: `process_gkr_input_air`\n173\t\n174\tChange the function signature:\n175\t```rust\n176\tfn process_gkr_input_air<HS: GpuHashScheme>(\n177\t    w: &GkrInputWorkItem<HS>,\n178\t    buffers: &mut GkrThreadBuffers,\n179\t) -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError>\n180\t```\n181\t\n182\tReplace per-AIR allocations:\n183\t\n184\t1. **`intermediates`** (lines 152-156): Remove the `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(...)` allocation. Use `&buffers.intermediates` directly. The pre-allocated buffer has `len >= TASK_SIZE * buffer_size` for all AIRs. For `is_global = false`, the kernel uses thread-local stack intermediates and ignores the global pointer, so the pre-allocated buffer is unused but harmless.\n185\t\n186\t2. **`d_public_values`** (lines 132-136): Keep the `if air_ctx.public_values.is_empty()` conditional to produce `DeviceBuffer::new()` (null pointer) for AIRs with no public values — this preserves the null-pointer semantics the kernel may rely on. For non-empty public values, use `air_ctx.public_values.copy_to(&mut buffers.public_values)?` (the existing `MemCopyH2D::copy_to` at `copy.rs:68-87`) which does `cudaMemcpyAsync` into the pre-allocated buffer without reallocation. The check `self.len() > dst.len()` passes because the source length ≤ max_public_values_len = pre-allocated length. Then pass `&buffers.public_values` to the kernel.\n187\t\n188\t3. **`d_partition_ptrs`** (lines 140-147): Use `buffers.partition_ptrs` instead of the old `d_partition_ptrs` parameter. Remove the resize check at line 144 since pre-allocated size is the max. Use `partition_ptrs.copy_to(&mut buffers.partition_ptrs)?` as before.\n189\t\n190\t4. **`tmp`** (lines 164-172): Use `buffers.tmp` instead of the old `tmp` parameter. Remove the resize check at line 166. Note: `buffers.tmp.len()` equals `max_tmp_len` (the max across all AIRs), which may be larger than `height * num_interactions` for the current AIR. This is safe because:\n191\t   - `frac_vector_scalar_multiply_ext_fp` (line 198) uses `tmp.len()` as the element count — processing extra elements is harmless (multiplies stale data by scalar, result is never read).\n192\t   - `frac_matrix_vertically_repeat` (line 200) uses explicit `num_interactions`, `lifted_height`, `height` parameters to determine read/write bounds, not `tmp.len()`.\n193\t   - However, to avoid unnecessary GPU work on stale elements, the `frac_vector_scalar_multiply_ext_fp` call should pass `(height * num_interactions) as u32` instead of `tmp.len() as u32`. This is a one-line change at line 198.\n194\t\n195\t### Change 5: Handle the single-threaded path consistently\n196\t\n197\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n198\t\n199\tFor `num_threads <= 1` (lines 264-269): use the first (only) `GkrThreadBuffers` from `thread_buffers`:\n200\t\n201\t```rust\n202\tif num_threads <= 1 {\n203\t    let mut bufs = thread_buffers.into_iter().next().unwrap();\n204\t    for w in &work_items {\n205\t        process_gkr_input_air(w, &mut bufs)?;\n206\t    }\n207\t}\n208\t```\n209\t\n210\t## Invariants\n211\t\n212\t1. **Correctness**: Every kernel call receives the same data as before. Buffers are overwritten before each kernel launch (`copy_to` for `partition_ptrs` and `public_values`, kernel writes for `intermediates`). Stale data from previous AIRs in pre-allocated buffers is always overwritten before use or ignored by the kernel.\n213\t\n214\t2. **Buffer sizing**: Pre-allocated buffers are sized to the maximum `len` across ALL AIRs in the segment. Every AIR's requirements fit. This is guaranteed by the pre-computation in Change 1.\n215\t\n216\t3. **Stream ordering**: Each thread's kernels execute sequentially on its per-thread CUDA stream (`cudaStreamPerThread`). Buffer reuse across AIRs within the same thread is safe because CUDA stream ordering guarantees kernel N finishes before kernel N+1 reads the buffer.\n217\t\n218\t4. **Cross-thread safety**: Each thread owns its own `GkrThreadBuffers` with its own GPU memory. No two threads share a buffer. The `SendPtr` pattern already used for `leaves_ptr` ensures output writes to non-overlapping regions.\n219\t\n220\t5. **Cross-stream allocation/free**: Buffers are allocated on the main thread's stream and freed on worker threads' streams (via `Drop` when the worker's closure exits, after `current_stream_sync()`). This is safe: the worker stream is synchronized before the buffer drops, and `d_free` tracks allocations by pointer, not by stream.\n221\t\n222\t6. **Public values null semantics**: AIRs with empty `public_values` still receive `DeviceBuffer::new()` (null pointer, len=0), preserving the current kernel's expectation.\n223\t\n224\t7. **APC 0 path**: When `work_items.len() < 100` (APC 0 with ~20 AIRs per segment), the single-threaded path is used with 1 buffer set. Pre-allocation overhead is negligible.\n225\t\n226\t8. **Memory budget**: If `total pre-allocation > 2GB`, the code reduces `num_threads` (halving) until the budget fits. In the worst case, falls back to 1 thread. Logged as a warning. The RTX 4090 has 24GB; typical max intermediates per thread is < 100MB, so 8 × 100MB = 800MB is well within budget.\n227\t\n228\t## Measurement Plan\n229\t\n230\t### Before changes\n231\tRun `run_pairing.sh` for APC {0, 100, 300}. Record:\n232\t- `prover.rap_constraints.logup_gkr.input_evals_time_ms` per segment (from metrics JSON)\n233\t- `prover.rap_constraints.logup_gkr_time_ms` per segment\n234\t- `stark_prove_excluding_trace_time_ms` per segment\n235\t- Full `spec.py` output for all three configs\n236\t\n237\t### After changes\n238\tRun the same benchmarks. Compare:\n239\t\n240\t**Primary metric**: `prover.rap_constraints.logup_gkr.input_evals_time_ms` (sum across segments)\n241\t- Target: < 400ms at APC 300 (vs current ~529ms, requiring >25% improvement)\n242\t\n243\t**Secondary metric**: `stark_prove_excluding_trace_time_ms` (spec.py \"STARK excl trace\")\n244\t- Target: < 1600ms at APC 300 (vs current ~1720ms, requiring >7% improvement)\n245\t\n246\t**No-regression**: STARK excl trace at APC 0 must not increase by more than 3% (< 2200ms).\n247\t\n248\t### Nsight validation\n249\tRun nsight on APC 300. Compare kernel concurrency:\n250\t- Before: `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel` instances on different streams show minimal temporal overlap\n251\t- After: instances on different streams show concurrent execution (visible in nsight timeline)\n252\t\n253\t### Correctness\n254\tAll three APC configs must complete prove+verify successfully.\n255\t\n256\t## Rollback Criteria\n257\t\n258\tRevert if ANY of:\n259\t1. STARK excl trace APC 300 improvement < 60ms (< 3.5% improvement)\n260\t2. STARK excl trace APC 0 regresses by > 65ms\n261\t3. GPU OOM at any APC config\n262\t4. Prove+verify fails at any config\n263\t5. GKR input eval wall time does not improve by at least 15% at APC 300\n264\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015up8qi3WWG2VDDCSpNUXqA","session_id":"39e8685b-d752-4f1d-86a0-2956436bbd44","uuid":"d2e49581-3676-40f8-90c4-0fe66fe0ff2d","timestamp":"2026-04-13T04:04:40.185Z"}
